rners Reference Grammar", "edition": "2", "author": "Thompson", "publisher": "NATIONAL AFRICAN LANGUAGES RES", "materialRequirementId": 2, "materialRequirement": "Optional", "priceNew": "33.50", "priceUsed": "18.75", "priceDigital": "", "isbn": "9781597030076", "courseCodeId": 282751}, {"internalId": 45217, "itemId": 27460, "title": "Swahilienglishenglishswahili Practical Dictionary", "edition": "", "author": "Awde", "publisher": "HIPPOCRENE BOOKS, INC", "materialRequirementId": 2, "materialRequirement": "Optional", "priceNew": "27.95", "priceUsed": "21.00", "priceDigital": "", "isbn": "9780781804806", "courseCodeId": 282751}]}], "unitsMinimum": 4, "unitsMaximum": 4, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["LEC"], "gradingBasis": "GRD", "gradingBasisShort": "Graded", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter grades only", "simpleCombinations": [], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "08/26/2021", "sessionEndDt": "12/18/2021", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": []}], "description": "Elementary Swahili provides a foundation in listening, speaking, reading, and writing the basic grammatical structures and vocabulary. No prior knowledge of the language is required. Swahili (Kiswahili) is spoken in the East and Central parts of Africa. It is an official and national language in Tanzania, and in Kenya. During a first semester course, students engage in short conversation and communicative tasks, such as, greetings, introduction, daily routines, shopping, etc. Students learn to comprehend short and simple utterances about topics pertaining to basic personal information and immediate setting in day to day life. A Swahili second semester increases your oral fluency, grammar, vocabulary, writing, reading, and listening skills. All listening exercises will aim at preparing students to speak. Be ready to actively participate in conversations, to express yourself orally, and write stories/compositions. Literature and Cultural materials are incorporated into the course, along with audio, video, and web-based materials.", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Fall.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AS", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": []}, {"strm": 2797, "crseId": 351028, "crseOfferNbr": 1, "subject": "SWAHL", "catalogNbr": "2101", "titleShort": "Intermediate Swahili I", "titleLong": "Intermediate Swahili I", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "SEM", "ssrComponentLong": "Seminar", "section": "101", "classNbr": 6112, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 2, "timeStart": "10:10AM", "timeEnd": "11:00AM", "startDt": "08/26/2021", "endDt": "12/07/2021", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "hpb36", "firstName": "Happiness", "middleName": "Patrick", "lastName": "Bulugu"}], "pattern": "MTWR", "facilityDescr": "Uris Hall G44", "bldgDescr": "Uris Hall", "facilityDescrshort": "URHG44", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "08/26/2021", "endDt": "12/18/2021", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": [], "materials": [{"internalId": 45221, "itemId": 28132, "title": "Tusome Kiswahili", "edition": "", "author": "Muaka", "publisher": "NATIONAL AFRICAN LANGUAGES RES", "materialRequirementId": 1, "materialRequirement": "Required", "priceNew": "53.50", "priceUsed": "35.75", "priceDigital": "", "isbn": "9781597030106", "courseCodeId": 282757}, {"internalId": 45220, "itemId": 41565, "title": "Swahili Learners Reference Grammar", "edition": "2", "author": "Thompson", "publisher": "NATIONAL AFRICAN LANGUAGES RES", "materialRequirementId": 2, "materialRequirement": "Optional", "priceNew": "33.50", "priceUsed": "18.75", "priceDigital": "", "isbn": "9781597030076", "courseCodeId": 282757}]}], "unitsMinimum": 4, "unitsMaximum": 4, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["SEM"], "gradingBasis": "GRD", "gradingBasisShort": "Graded", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter grades only", "simpleCombinations": [], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "08/26/2021", "sessionEndDt": "12/18/2021", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": []}], "description": "Intermediate Swahili levels I and II in general impart speaking, listening, reading, and writing skills beyond Swahili elementary level to participate with ease and confidence in familiar topics and exchange information on unfamiliar topics. Students are assigned communicative tasks such as respond to a situation with a short text and take part in a discussion after viewing short video clips and prompts to elicit speaking and listening competence and cultural awareness responses beyond elementary level. The language and cultural scenarios practiced are designed to help students demonstrate language responses beyond familiar topics, and to feel comfortable conversing with Swahili native speakers, as well as to blend in and feel\u00a0welcomed as part of the community while exploring different topics such as acquaintanceship, relationships, health, festivals, education, sports, housing, politics, commerce, travel, etc. Short stories are used to depict cultural aspects such as cultural expressions, proverbs, sayings, and riddles. Literature and cultural materials are incorporated into the course, along with audio-visual and web-based material. In this course, students have an opportunity to participate in language conversation outside the classroom and explore the opportunities for study abroad in East Africa. Swahili Elementary I and II are prerequisite for this course. By the end of this course, students should be able to reach proficiency level Intermediate High according to the American Council on the Teaching of Foreign Languages (ACTFL) www.actfl.org.", "catalogBreadth": "(GB)", "catalogDistr": "", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "Satisfies Option 1.", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Fall.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "Prerequisite: SWAHL 1100\u00a0and SWAHL 1101.", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AS", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": []}]}, "message": null, "meta": {"copyright": "Cornell University, Office of the University Registrar", "rosterDttm": "2022-02-07T19:27:30-0500", "catalogDttm": "2022-02-07T19:14:23-0500"}}